<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Consul</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as consulship</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A consul was the highest elected magistrate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, two being elected annually to command armies and preside over the government. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the office survived as a prestigious honour without real power. The consulship symbolised Roman continuity long after the emperors had taken control.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emperor Anastasius granted him an honorary <b>consulship</b>, and in his last years Clovis eliminated the rulers of the other Frankish groups, such as Sigibert of Cologne, made Paris his chief residence and summoned a church council at Orléans in 511, the year he died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jugurthine War exposed corruption in the Roman Senate, gave Gaius Marius the <b>consulship</b> of 107 BCE, during which he enrolled men without property in the legions, and launched the career of Lucius Cornelius Sulla.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Octavian, granted the honorific Augustus by the Senate in 27 BCE, became Rome's first emperor while carefully preserving the outward forms of republican government, retaining the Senate, the <b>consulship</b> and the traditional magistracies as instruments of legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…and plebeians, who in the Conflict of the Orders won the tribunate after the first secession of 494 BCE, written law in the Twelve Tables of 451–450 BCE, access to the <b>consulship</b> from 366 BCE and binding force for plebeian resolutions under the Lex Hortensia of 287 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Conflict of the Orders, which ended in 287 BCE, plebeians won access to the magistracies, and a new nobility, the nobiles, formed from the patrician and plebeian families whose ancestors had held the <b>consulship</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…the city: tribunes of their own after the first secession in 494 BCE, a written code, the Twelve Tables, traditionally of 451–450 BCE, access to the <b>consulship</b> from 367 BCE, and in 287 BCE the lex Hortensia, which gave the decisions of their assembly the force of law.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
